# Dynamic properties of a flexible metal-organic framework exhibiting a unique “picture frame”-like crystal morphology

Kenji Sumida1,2, Nao Horike1, and Shuhei Furukawa1 $\boxed{\boxtimes}$
1 Institute for Integrated Cell-Material Sciences (WPI-iCeMS), Kyoto University, Yoshida, Sakyo-ku, Kyoto 606-8501, Japan   
2 Centre for Advanced Nanomaterials, School of Physical Sciences, University of Adelaide, SA 5005, Australia   
$\circledcirc$ Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2020   
Received: 28 February 2020 / Revised: 3 July 2020 / Accepted: 19 July 2020
## ABSTRACT
The precise control of the crystal morphology of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) enables optimization of its adsorptive properties, as well as enables better integration within functional devices. However, the influence of such modifications on the dynamic properties of flexible MOFs is poorly understood. Here, we report the synthesis of a series of $\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathsf{b d c})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})$ $({\mathsf{b d c}}^{2-}=$ 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate; bpy $\mathit{\Pi}=4{,}4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) crystals having an unusual picture frame-like morphology that results from a restriction in the quantity of bpy pillars added to the reaction mixture during the intercalation of the $\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathsf{b d c})_{2}(\mathsf{M e O H})_{2}$ layers. The width of the frames is found to correlate with the quantity of bpy, and importantly, causes the dynamic properties of the resulting $\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathsf{b d c})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})$ material to vary between rigid, elastic, and shape memory modes. In all, the results demonstrate the potential for the properties of MOFs to be optimized via subtle manipulations in the crystal morphology rather than changes in the overall material composition.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, morphology control, framework flexibility, shape-memory effect, gas sorption
## Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have received considerable attention in recent years due to their high surface area, chemical tunability, and their potential to be deployed as functional materials in applications such as gas storage, molecular separations, and drug delivery [1, 2]. Although the ability to rationally select the metal and organic components that construct these extended, crystalline networks enables a higher degree of control over the physical and chemical properties relative to other solid-state materials, the manipulation of higher-order features such as the crystal size and morphology is becoming increasingly investigated as a pathway for further optimization of the materials for specific applications [3–11]. For example, reductions in the crystal size can facilitate more rapid diffusion of guest molecules into the pores, which can be beneficial for increasing the rate of adsorptive phenomena [12]. Meanwhile, control of the crystal morphology can significantly modify the properties of the external surface through greater contributions from certain crystal facets, which can be of benefit for size-based discrimination of molecules [13] or surface-based catalytic reactions [14].
The exquisite control over the crystal size can also lead to new materials phenomena in MOFs that exhibit dynamic structural properties in response to external stimuli [15–20]. For example, the flexible $\mathrm{\calCu_{2}(b d c)_{2}(b p y)}$ (Fig. 1(a); ${\mathrm{bd}}{\mathrm{c}}^{2-}=$ 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate; $\mathsf{b p y}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) framework is a two-fold interpenetrated material that exhibits a remarkable size-dependence with respect to its dynamic properties induced by the adsorption and desorption of guest molecules [21].
Here, a typical, bulk-scale synthesis of the material yields $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ sized crystals that display an elastic flexibility between a guestfree closed form and guest-filled open form (see Fig. 2). However, downsizing of the crystals results in the emergence of a third, guest-free open form which could be restored to the closed pore form via heating, which is a phenomenon reminiscent of the behavior of certain “shape memory” metal alloys and polymers whose original structure can be restored via the application of thermal energy. In this case, the ability of the guest-free open pore form to be sustained at smaller crystal sizes is associated with a stabilization of this phase relative to the closed form, which results in a larger energy barrier being associated with the structural change. Although similar phenomena have recently been reported in other structurallyrelated frameworks [22], examples remain rare and insights into the physical and determinants of this type of behavior remain limited [21–23].
Since the bulk structural flexibility of any MOF system stems from the propagation of changes in the structure of the metal nodes and/or the organic linker to higher-order length scales, the impact of molecular-level changes on the shape memory phenomenon in the $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{bdc})_{2}(\mathbf{bpy})$ system is expected to yield new insights into the origins of its unique, dynamic behavior. More specifically, among the components that construct the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})$ framework, the bpy unit is considered to be particularly important in influencing the structural flexibility due to its structural role in pillaring the square grid layers. Therefore, we hypothesized that a modification in the site occupancy of the bpy pillars within the structure below full occupancy could influence the dynamic properties of the framework via a reduction in the connectivity between the layers. Herein, we report the synthesis and dynamic properties of a series of $\mathrm{Cu_{2}(b d c)_{2}(b p y)}$ materials synthesized using systematic variations in the amount of bpy available as pillaring ligands. By taking advantage of its two-step synthetic protocol, which entails the formation of two-dimensional ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}({\mathrm{bd}}{\mathrm{c}})_{2}$ layers, followed by the insertion of bpy linkers between the layers, we synthesized a new, picture frame-like crystal morphology of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})$ , the width of which is directly correlated to the quantity of bpy pillars. The flexibility of the system is strongly influenced by the frame width, which highlights the importance of controlling both the molecular-level composition and higher-order features of the crystals (Fig. 1). In all, the results suggest that subtle manipulations to flexible MOFs may impart considerable changes to their dynamic properties, which represents an important consideration given the interest in deploying these types of materials in real-world applications.
## 2 Results and discussion
Prior to discussing the synthesis and characterization of the new materials, we briefly review the dynamic properties of bulk $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\ b d c)_{2}(\ b p y)}$ and its downsized variants. The structure of $\mathrm{Cu_{2}(b d c)_{2}(b p y)}$ is shown in Fig. 1(a). The framework comprises dinuclear copper paddlewheel units that are bridged by $\operatorname{bdc}^{2-}$ linkers to form two-dimensional square grid layers of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{2}$ (Fig. 1(b)). The axial positions on the paddlewheel units are pillared by bpy donors, resulting in a three-dimensional, two-fold interpenetrated structure in which the pillars are threaded through the square grids of adjacent layers. In the bulk form of the material (crystal size: $>1\upmu\mathrm{m}\rangle$ ) and size-modulated forms above ${300}\mathrm{nm}$ , the compound exhibits a reversible (elastic) structural transition between an open pore (solvated) and closed pore (desolvated) form. As the crystal size is reduced, the material begins to adopt a third, guest-free open form upon guest removal, and the contribution of this phase increases until a crystal size of $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ , at which the sample predominantly consists of this unusual phase after evacuation of the guest from the pores. The original closed pore form of the material can be recovered upon heating of the sample to $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ demonstrating that the guest-free open form exists as a metastable state unique to the material following crystal downsizing [21]. This new adsorption cycle can be recognized as a “shape-memory effect” in a MOF.
## 2.1 Synthesis of frame-like crystals of $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{bdc})_{2}(\mathbf{bpy})$
The synthesis of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})$ samples was carried out using a procedure adapted from the preparation of bulk materials [21]. In a typical reaction, a methanol solution containing 1,4-benzenedicarboxoylic acid was added to a mixture of copper acetate monohydrate and acetic acid as modulators ( ${\bf\dot{\boldsymbol{r}}}=10.$ , 30, and 50, where $r=$ [acetic acid]/[copper acetate]). The mixture was stirred vigorously for three days, after which a sky-blue precipitate emerged corresponding to the two-dimensional layered compound, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{2}$ . Thereafter, a methanol solution of bpy was added to the reaction mixture using variable pillar ratios $(p)$ of $p=0.2,0.3,0.4,0.5,0.75$ , and 1.0 (where $\boldsymbol{p}$ is expressed as the ratio of the stoichiometry of bpy to $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{2})$ . Further stirring of the solution for two days allowed bpy to intercalate into the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{2}$ layers and yielded the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})$ framework in the form of an emerald-green precipitate, which was washed with methanol, and dried in vacuo at room temperature. Note that, in the following discussion, the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})$ samples will be collectively referred to as 1, or individually as $\mathbf{1}{-}\mathbf{\mathit{p}}$ , where $\boldsymbol{p}$ is the pillar ratio described above.
This data described above afforded two possible interpretations of the composition of the individual crystals, namely (i) the homogeneous distribution of pillar ligand within a crystal and to form an overall crystal composition of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bd}c)_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})_{P}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{2-2P},$ or (ii) the heterogeneous distribution of bpy close to the edge of initial layer crystals leads to the formation of fully pillared layers within the frame-like crystals with the presence of the two-dimensional (non-pillared) $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{2}$ compound within the same crystal. To test these possibilities, a solution of a sample prepared using $p=0.5$ was charged with a further 0.5 equivalents of bpy in a secondary pillaring step. This resulted in a compound with a plate-like morphology and properties indistinguishable from a bulk sample $(p=1.0)$ . Therefore, we can surmise the following regarding the mechanism of pillaring in the frame-like crystals. As depicted in Fig. 4, the pillars begin to insert between the square grid layers from the external surfaces of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{2}$ crystals, resulting in a peripheral region that is fully pillared, and a non-pillared central region when $\smash{p<1.0}$ . Then, upon application of vacuum to dry the material, the macroscopic stress resulting from the presence of two different frameworks in a single crystal results in separation of the two phases, resulting in 1 as a frame-like morphology. Small clusters of plate-like crystals were also occasionally observed in the microscopy experiments, which further supports the elimination of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{2}$ from the pillared crystals. Meanwhile, because the partially pillared crystals are stable while in solution, the secondary pillaring step enables the full pillaring of the original crystals (producing the rightmost state in Fig. 4) without the emergence of the frame-like crystal morphology.
## 2.2 Dynamic behavior of 1 prepared using variable pillar concentrations $(p)$
The frame-like crystals displayed in Fig. 3 were subjected to a suite of characterization techniques, including powder X-ray diffraction and adsorption experiments to better understand the influence of the crystal morphology on the framework flexibility. The powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction patterns for 1 in a solvated state (methanol), after drying at room temperature, $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , and $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are shown in Fig. 5. All samples exhibited the open-pore form in the solvated state due to the presence of solvent molecules in the pores. Upon drying the samples at room temperature, plate-shaped crystals synthesized using a stoichiometric quantity of bpy pillars $(p=1.0)$ exhibited an elastic flexibility and immediately adopted the closed-pore form. Although a small proportion of the crystals prepared at $p=0.5$ and 0.75 also exhibited this behavior, the samples containing $p<1.0$ (and exhibiting the frame-like crystal morphology) predominantly maintained the open-pore form after solvent removal. Further heating of the samples to $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ led to little change in the diffraction patterns for the samples (except for a slight increase in the content of the closed-pore form for $\begin{array}{r}{p=0.4\AA,}\end{array}$ , suggesting that the guest-free open-pore form was preferred by each of the samples prepared using $p<1.0$ . Interestingly, thermal treatment at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (which induces a transition from the open-pore to closed-pore form in downsized variants of fully pillared $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{{bdc})}_{2}(\mathrm{{bpy}))}$ resulted in only a minor increase in the proportion of closed-pore form crystals. This demonstrates that the samples prepared at $\smash{p<1.0}$ have a strong tendency to be rigid (i.e. remaining in the open-pore form), with a gradual increase in the proportion of shape-memory crystals as the quantity of pillars (and therefore, the thickness of the frames and the crystal solid volume) is increased beyond $p=0.5$ . This trend is similar to that observed in the case of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpe})$ $\mathrm{bpe}=1{,}2$ -bis(4-pyridyl)ethylene) compound, which is an elastic framework in the bulk phase that exhibits shape-memory at intermediate crystal sizes, and rigidity at the smaller crystal sizes [21].
## 2.3 Dynamic behavior of 1 prepared using variations in modulator concentration (r)
To further probe the influence of the crystal morphology on the dynamic behavior of 1, samples with the smaller crystal sizes were prepared using two additional modulator concentrations 1 ${\bf\dot{\boldsymbol{r}}}=10$ and 30) at pillar ratios of $p=0.5$ and 0.75. These values were selected to give the best possible comparison with the data described above, specifically with the aim of elucidating whether the enhanced rigidity of the frame-like crystals is affected by the crystal size. As shown in the TEM images displayed in Fig. 6, the use of $r=10$ and 30 gives crystal sizes of approximately 50 and $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , respectively, while the reduced size of the crystals leads to the frame-like morphology becoming less apparent throughout the samples.
A comparison of the powder X-ray diffraction patterns for the size-modulated samples under the same conditions tested above is presented in Fig. 7. The dynamic behavior of the samples prepared at $p=0.5$ showed relatively little sensitivity to the crystal size, in that the crystals remain primarily rigid across all three size regimes. Meanwhile, at $p=0.75$ , although the samples clearly showed a mixture of rigid and shape memory crystals, the smaller $50\mathrm{nm}$ crystals exhibited a greater proportion of crystals of the latter type as demonstrated by the marked increase in the intensity of diffraction peaks corresponding to the closed-pore form upon heating the sample to $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The increased stability of the guest-free open-pore form for smaller crystals is consistent with our previous observations from the downsizing of crystals prepared using $p=1.0$ [21], but the emergence of rigidity at larger crystal sizes is unique to the frame-like crystals studied here.
## 2.4 Geometric analysis of frame-like crystals
The experimental evidence for the frame-like crystals containing of a full occupancy of bpy pillars allows an idealized geometry for the crystals to be calculated at each value of $\boldsymbol{p}$ (see Fig. 8). Here, the frame width is given by the length $\mathbf{\xi}_{l,\mathbf{\xi}}$ which is assumed to be uniform for the entire periphery of the crystal, and the number of repeating units in the $a b$ -plane of the crystal can be readily approximated based on the unit cell dimensions of the single-crystal structure. The tendency toward rigidity in the crystals increases as the value of l decreases relative to the overall crystal size $(\boldsymbol{w})$ . In other words, the crystals have a propensity toward rigidity as the quantity of bpy $(p)$ decreases. This suggests that there is decreased cooperativity to enable the structural transition from the open-pore form to the closed-pore form when the frame is thinner relative to the overall crystal dimensions (i.e. when the quantity $l/w$ is decreased). This is consistent with the relatively higher stability of the open-pore form in downsized crystals studied previously, although the rigidity observed here suggests that the morphology imparts an even greater stabilization to the extent that it prohibits a transition to the closed-pore form. This is presumably due to the decreased number of propagation pathways for the structural transition to occur once the central portion of the crystal is removed, resulting in a larger energy barrier that is not overcome within the temperature range employed here.
## 3 Conclusion
The foregoing has described the synthesis and characterization of a flexible metal-organic framework exhibiting a new, frame-like morphology. As discussed above, the morphology imparts a significant influence on the flexibility of the system relative to those prepared in a conventional manner with a full equivalent of the bpy pillar ligand. The results demonstrate that considerably different dynamic properties can be obtained from the same framework when the structuralization of the material is modified, which forms an important consideration when potentially manipulating the physical form of metal-organic frameworks to suit specific applications. It is expected that more studies of this type will uncover an even greater range of structuralizationdependent dynamic behavior, and an enhanced understanding of this area will likely allow such phenomena to be used advantageously to further optimize the properties of the materials.
## 4 Experimental section
## 4.1 General considerations
Unless otherwise noted, all reagents were obtained from commercial vendors and used as received. While all syntheses were carried out in the air, the desolvated forms of each of the compounds were handled and stored in a nitrogen-filled glove box. Methanol adsorption measurements were carried out on a BELSORP-Max adsorption analyser (BEL Japan, Inc.) equipped with a constant temperature bath. Powder X-ray diffraction patterns were performed using a Smartlab X-ray Diffractometer (Rigaku Corp.) equipped with a Cu $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ source.
## 4.2 Field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM)
SEM images were collected using a JEOL JSM-7001F4 electron microscope. The samples were evacuated to remove any residual solvent molecules and attached to a $13.5~\mathrm{mm}$ substrate using double-sided carbon tape. The samples were then coated with osmium nanoparticles to a thickness of $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ and transferred to the SEM instrument. The images were collected using an emission voltage between $10{-}15\mathrm{kV}.$
## 4.3 TEM
The TEM observations were performed with a Hitachi H-7100 transmission electron microscopy system operating at $100\mathrm{kV}.$ The TEM samples were prepared by dispersing the precipitates in tetrahydrofuran (THF)/methanol, followed by depositing the solution dropwise onto a carbon-coated TEM grid.
## 4.4 Synthesis of $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}p$
A solution of terephthalic acid $(34~\mathrm{mg};0.20~\mathrm{mmol})$ in methanol 1 $\mathrm{32~mL})$ ) was added to a mixture consisting of $\mathrm{Cu(OAc)_{2}}{\cdot}\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ $(40\ \mathrm{\mg};\ 0.20\ \mathrm{\mmol})$ , methanol $(12.5~\mathrm{mL})$ and acetic acid $(575~\mathrm{mL})$ , and the mixture was stirred for three days at room temperature to generate $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{MeOH})$ as a sky-blue suspension. A solution containing bpy (3.1, 4.7, 6.2, 7.8, 11.7, or $15.6\mathrm{mg}$ for $p=0.2,0.3,0.4,0.5,0.75.$ , and 1.0, respectively) in methanol ( $\mathrm{10~mL)}$ was subsequently added, and the mixture was stirred at room temperature for a further two days. An emerald-green precipitate was collected and washed with methanol three sonication and centrifugation cycles. After isolation, the sample was further dried at room temperature under a vacuum and activated at up to $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ prior to adsorption analysis.
